---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const gh = "https://github.com";
const repo = `${gh}/vignesh-chaturvedi/zerotohype`;
/** Merged pull requests by the author in a repository, as GitHub lists them. */
const merged = (r: string) => `${gh}/${r}/pulls?q=is%3Apr+is%3Amerged+author%3Avignesh-chaturvedi`;
---

<Base title="About" description="What Zero to HYPE is, who writes it, how its facts are checked, and what it does and doesn't track.">
  <div style="display: contents" data-pagefind-body data-pagefind-meta="kind:About">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>A field guide, kept <em>honest.</em></h1>
  </header>
  <article class="prose">
    <p>
      Zero to HYPE is a free roadmap for developers building on Hyperliquid. Each topic links the
      official documentation and the best tutorials others have written, then adds what those
      leave out: worked formulas, labs you can run in the browser, and notes from running real
      services against the API.
    </p>

    <h2>How facts are checked</h2>
    <p>
      Hyperliquid changes its API often and documents some of it late, so the site keeps track of
      what it says and when it last checked.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Every lesson shows the date</strong> its facts were last checked against the live
        API. The dot beside it turns from green to amber after 14 days, and to red after 30.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A bot watches the API and the docs.</strong> Every 6 hours it compares mainnet,
        testnet and the docs with what it saw last, and records the differences in the{" "}
        <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, which you can also <a href="/changelog/rss.xml">follow by RSS</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A lesson says when its sources change.</strong> If a docs page it relies on is edited
        after the lesson was checked, a note at the top says so until it's checked again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Claims about signing and errors are tested on testnet</strong>, with keys made for
        the purpose that hold nothing and can't change anything. Scripts in the{" "}
        <a href={repo}>repository</a> run those tests again, and every SDK example was run the same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Settlements are recomputed, not quoted.</strong> The{" "}
        <a href="/labs/settlement/">settlement explorer</a> works out real HIP-4 results from prices
        hyperkeeper recorded, and shows where its answer and the venue's settlement differ.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={`${gh}/vignesh-chaturvedi`}>Vignesh Chaturvedi</a>. On Hyperliquid, that's{" "}
      <a href={merged("infinitefield/hypersdk")}>pull requests merged into hypersdk</a>, the Rust
      SDK, adding HIP-3 deployer settings, HIP-3* and HIP-4 actions and native trailing stops, and
      fixing how addresses are signed; <a href={merged("infinitefield/yawc")}>fixes to yawc</a>, the
      WebSocket library it uses; and <a href={`${gh}/vignesh-chaturvedi/hyperkeeper`}>hyperkeeper</a>,
      an open-source operations tool for HIP-3 and HIP-4 deployers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Elsewhere, there's merged work in <a href={merged("anza-xyz/agave")}>Agave</a>, the Solana
      validator, in <a href={merged("rust-lang/libc")}>rust-lang/libc</a>, and in{" "}
      <a href={merged("obi1kenobi/cargo-semver-checks")}>cargo-semver-checks</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every lesson has a link to edit it on GitHub. To report something wrong or out of date,{" "}
      <a href={`${repo}/issues/new`}>open an issue</a>; it's the quickest way to get it fixed, and
      the fix shows up in the site's history.
    </p>

    <h2>What the site stores</h2>
    <p>
      The site sets no cookies. Visits are counted by Cloudflare Web Analytics, which uses none
      either. Your theme, your progress through the roadmap, your changelog filter and your code
      language are kept in your own browser, only once you choose them, and are never sent
      anywhere. The labs and the live figures call Hyperliquid's public API straight from your
      browser.
    </p>

    <h2>Independent</h2>
    <p>
      Zero to HYPE is not affiliated with or endorsed by Hyperliquid. Labs only ever use testnet
      and keys generated in your browser; never paste a real private key into any site,
      including this one.
    </p>

    <h2>Licences</h2>
    <p>Code is MIT-licensed. Lessons and other content are CC BY 4.0: reuse them with credit.</p>
  </article>
  </div>
</Base>
